  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe rest of today through Monday...\n\nA shortwave in the flow will bring a 20-50% chance of showers and\nthunderstorms to the mountains and foothills through this evening.\nNo severe storms are expected.\n\nUpper level ridging continues to build over the region into\nThursday, allowing highs to increase from low 80s today to low to\nmid 90s Thursday. Occasional shortwaves in the flow will continue\nto provide enough energy for isolated (10-40% chance) afternoon \nand evening showers and thunderstorms Wednesday. By Thursday, a \nbetter push of moisture (PWATs around 130-160% of normal) will \nallow more scattered showers and thunderstorms (30-60% chance) to \ndevelop. There is potential for some stronger storms to develop \nThursday afternoon, as some areas may see CAPE values around \n800-1200 J/kg and shear around 30-45 kts. \n\nFriday into Saturday, an upper level trough approaches from the \nPacific Northwest, bringing increased moisture (PWATs around \n150-180% of normal) and an increased chance (40-70%) for showers \nand thunderstorms. This system will also bring cooling \ntemperatures, with highs decreasing to upper 70s to mid 80s by \nSaturday.\n\nOn Sunday, the trough exits to the east and the increased \nmoisture is cut off. Another system just off the west coast will \nsend occasional weak shortwaves through the flow, allowing a low \nchance (10-30%) for showers and thunderstorms Sunday into early \nnext week. Highs will remain in the upper 70s to mid 80s into\nearly next week. Archer",
